tag_template.description:
Git tag template used to tag and identify releases (supports templates). The first rung that applies wins:

1. this field, when set;
2. `defaults.crates.tag_template`;
3. `<tag.tag_prefix>{{ Version }}` when the repo sets `tag.tag_prefix`;
4. `v{{ Version }}` when the root `Cargo.toml` declares `[workspace.package].version` (a lockstep workspace);
5. the `<name>-v{{ Version }}` convention.

Rungs 3 and 4 never apply to a config with a `workspaces:` list. Rungs 2–4 are folded into this field at config load; read the resolved family through `tag_family_template()` and do not touch this field directly.

retention.description:
Retention policy for nightly releases on GitHub. Generalizes `keep_single_release` (which is `keep_last: 1`): keeps the N newest nightly releases matching the nightly tag/name and deletes the rest (releases + the tags anodizer created for them). Operates on `publish_repo` when set.

The number kept resolves in this order:

1. `retention` set: `keep_last`, with `0` read as `1`;
2. `retention` unset and `keep_single_release: true`: `1`;
3. otherwise no old nightly is deleted.

provider.description:
Explicit publish target: the SCM provider (`github`, `gitlab` or `gitea`) whose `release.<provider>` block the publisher uses. The provider is the first of these that gives an answer:

1. `release.provider`;
2. the top-level `force_token`;
3. the `ANODIZER_FORCE_TOKEN` environment variable, or `GORELEASER_FORCE_TOKEN` when `ANODIZER_FORCE_TOKEN` is unset (`github`, `gitlab` or `gitea` in any letter case; any other value gives no answer);
4. `GITLAB_TOKEN` set in the environment: GitLab;
5. `GITEA_TOKEN` set in the environment: Gitea;
6. GitHub.

Set it for cross-platform publishing, where the source repo lives on one provider (e.g. GitLab) and releases go to another (e.g. GitHub), or whenever more than one forge token is in the environment.

```yaml
release:
  provider: github
  github:
    owner: my-org
    name: my-app
```
